{"@context":{"lean":"lean:","block":"block:","imports":{"@type":"@id"}},"@graph":[{"@id":"lean:module:QOU.Archimedean.GramDetRatioCongruence","@type":"LeanModule","name":"QOU.Archimedean.GramDetRatioCongruence","imports":["lean:module:Init","lean:module:Mathlib.LinearAlgebra.Matrix.Determinant.Basic"],"declarations":3,"chunks":1},{"@id":"lean:QOU.Archimedean.gramDetRatio_indep_of_basis","@type":"LeanDeclaration","name":"QOU.Archimedean.gramDetRatio_indep_of_basis","kind":"theorem","line":72,"sourceLink":"https://github.com/litlfred/qou/blob/7341d18daa5d103cc9fa41bebdf054eda96ad3d7/folio/quantum-observable-universe/lean/QOU/Archimedean/GramDetRatioCongruence.lean#L72-L81","doc":"The ratio is the *same* for any two admissible bases: it is a function\nof the pair of forms alone.  This is the statement the `.md`'s \"the choice\nof basis for the rank-2 sublattice is not fixed\" would have to contradict\nin order to be a source of freedom in `κ`. ","signatureChunk":0},{"@id":"lean:QOU.Archimedean.gramDetRatio_congr_invariant","@type":"LeanDeclaration","name":"QOU.Archimedean.gramDetRatio_congr_invariant","kind":"theorem","line":61,"sourceLink":"https://github.com/litlfred/qou/blob/7341d18daa5d103cc9fa41bebdf054eda96ad3d7/folio/quantum-observable-universe/lean/QOU/Archimedean/GramDetRatioCongruence.lean#L61-L70","doc":"**The ratio of two Gram determinants is congruence-invariant.**\n\nIf both forms are transported by the *same* invertible `B`, the\n`(det B)^2` cancels and the ratio is unchanged.  No hypothesis is placed\non `B` beyond `det B ≠ 0` — in particular it need not be unimodular, so a\nchange of sublattice does not move the ratio either. ","signatureChunk":0},{"@id":"lean:QOU.Archimedean.det_congr_eq","@type":"LeanDeclaration","name":"QOU.Archimedean.det_congr_eq","kind":"theorem","line":53,"sourceLink":"https://github.com/litlfred/qou/blob/7341d18daa5d103cc9fa41bebdf054eda96ad3d7/folio/quantum-observable-universe/lean/QOU/Archimedean/GramDetRatioCongruence.lean#L53-L59","doc":"Congruence scales a determinant by `(det B)^2`.\n\nOver any commutative ring: `det (Bᵀ * G * B) = (det B)^2 * det G`. ","signatureChunk":0}]}